 "result": "All in `src/tessel/views.py`. This is a Flask-style design with renamed methods.\n\n**Becoming a view function**\n- `VerbView` subclasses `View` (line 138). You subclass `VerbView` and define `get()`/`post()`.\n- `VerbView.__init_subclass__` (line 165) scans the class for names in `http_method_funcs` (line 11). It sets `cls.methods` to the uppercased matches, such as `{\"GET\", \"POST\"}`, unless the class defines `methods` itself.\n- `View.to_view_func(name, ...)` (line 86) is a classmethod that builds the plain function `view(**kwargs)`.\n  - By default it instantiates the class on every request. With `init_every_request = False`, it reuses a single instance.\n  - It calls `active_app.ensure_sync(self.run_view)(**kwargs)`.\n  - It applies any `cls.decorators`.\n  - It attaches `view_class`, `methods` and `provide_automatic_options` to the function.\n- You register it with `app.attach_route_rule(path, view_func=Cls.to_view_func(\"name\"))`. The route reads `view.methods` to know which HTTP methods to accept.\n\n**Routing to get()/post()**\n- `VerbView.run_view` (line 182) does the dispatch:\n  - It looks up `getattr(self, request.method.lower())`.\n  - If the request is `HEAD` and there is no `head` handler, it falls back to `get`.\n  - It calls the handler via `active_app.ensure_sync(meth)(**kwargs)`, passing the URL variables.",
